WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.850
Speaker 1: It is great to welcome to the program
today, Heidi Bogosian, who's in New York City

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:10.170
based attorney and author, whose latest book
is I Have Nothing to Hide and 20 Other Myths

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:12.460
About Surveillance and Privacy.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:13.490
It's really great having you on.

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:14.490
I appreciate your time.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.110
Speaker 2: Thank you, David.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:17.110
Great to be here.

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:21.130
Speaker 1: So let's start historically a little
bit if we can.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:29.530
Was there sort of a surveillance state before
9/11 and the Patriot Act and the bulk surveillance

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:32.410
that we've learned about over the last decade
or so?

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:36.190
What was there an apparatus that predated
that and to what extent?

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:42.399
Speaker 2: There has always been a surveillance
apparatus in the United States and the justification

00:00:42.399 --> 00:00:45.170
for it remains the same.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:53.420
It's largely based on the government's theory
that we need to be monitored to protect national

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.070
security.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:01:02.909
And I think what we've seen with the events
of 9/11, but also as the digital age has really

00:01:02.909 --> 00:01:09.410
taken over our lives, we've seen that surveillance
apparatus on steroids in effect.

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:15.580
And the ways that we can be monitored are
just about as many as one can imagine.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:22.810
And we're seeing so many levels of creativity
to do that, spying on children, to just invading

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:24.850
all aspects of our daily lives.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:30.880
Speaker 1: Let's talk about some of the methods,
I guess, because people may not be familiar

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:31.880
with all of them.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:37.340
I think metadata is a term that many more
people now are familiar with maybe than they

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:43.490
used to be in this concept of, well, even
without the content of a phone call, a list

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:49.460
of the numbers you've interacted with, who
can be very revealing and invasive when it

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:52.050
when it comes to observing your activities.

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:56.050
But can you talk a little bit about the breadth
of these techniques and technologies that

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:57.050
are in place?

00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:02.950
Speaker 2: An easy way to look at the extent
to which we are monitored is to think of the

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:06.060
gold rush of the early days of this nation.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:15.180
We're in a data rush right now, and data represents
essentially the crude oil that fuels our digital

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:16.760
economy.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:21.019
So every time you log on, we have third party.

00:02:21.019 --> 00:02:26.329
We have data aggregators, which is more than
a two hundred million dollar billion dollar

00:02:26.329 --> 00:02:28.219
business in this country.

00:02:28.219 --> 00:02:36.430
So if you think of it as your personal information
is like nuggets of gold that others want to

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:44.500
reap, collect as much as they can, essentially
without the laws, sort of like the property

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:48.659
laws that didn't exist when we had the the
early gold rush.

00:02:48.659 --> 00:02:57.049
Our legislators have not kept up with tech
advances so that Facebook, for example, just

00:02:57.049 --> 00:03:03.450
announced that it was putting the pause on
developing an app, an Instagram type app for

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:11.720
kids right up, which really habituate to not
only young kids to become loyal users of that

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:16.980
app, but it allows third parties to gather
personal information.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:23.790
It may be just what sites they visit, what
preferences they have when they purchase something

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:26.439
online, or when they ask their parents to
buy something.

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:34.739
Each of those kernels, when put together,
can give a really accurate profile of our

00:03:34.739 --> 00:03:35.739
lives.

00:03:35.739 --> 00:03:37.269
And you mentioned made it metadata.

00:03:37.269 --> 00:03:43.120
Well, metadata is defined very loosely as
information about information.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:47.209
So it's sort of when a photo might have been
taken.

00:03:47.209 --> 00:03:54.989
It's kind of embedded in all of the documents
that we get online that when captured in large

00:03:54.989 --> 00:04:01.879
quantities and having algorithms applied to
search for patterns in that can really tell

00:04:01.879 --> 00:04:04.180
a lot about who we are.

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:11.769
For example, if I went to a cancer doctor
five times in one week or an abortion provider,

00:04:11.769 --> 00:04:17.989
those that information is then sold to others,
including government agencies, but largely

00:04:17.989 --> 00:04:24.280
marketers and for profit entities who then
design ads to target us to buy more of a similar

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:30.030
product or to send us ads about cancer treatments
and medications.

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:38.289
The problem is when you get faulty algorithms,
as we so often do, and one example was drone

00:04:38.289 --> 00:04:45.229
strikes that this country launched in Afghanistan
and other countries from 2006 on, there were

00:04:45.229 --> 00:04:50.810
flawed algorithms, which means that in that
case there were lethal consequences.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:54.830
People who really shouldn't have been on a
terrorist watch list, for example, may have

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:58.090
been killed in drone strikes, innocent individuals.

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:00.419
Speaker 1: Right.

00:05:00.419 --> 00:05:08.111
There's kind of two layers on which we could
talk about the security aspect of the surveillance

00:05:08.111 --> 00:05:09.111
state.

00:05:09.111 --> 00:05:13.490
One would be, does it make us safer?

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:18.860
And if it did, we could then have a debate
about the tradeoffs and whether that's worthwhile.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:21.550
So maybe we can start with the first piece.

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:27.389
Do we know that these techniques put in place
starting around 9/11 and going forward?

00:05:27.389 --> 00:05:29.849
Does this increase safety in any measurable
way?

00:05:29.849 --> 00:05:35.320
Speaker 2: There's been no evidence to prove
that it does increase safety.

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:42.250
And that's really a tragedy because I think
millions of Americans may think they're doing

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:45.319
the right thing by saying, I have nothing
to hide.

00:05:45.319 --> 00:05:48.060
You can have my data.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:56.830
But I think it was the Navy War College issued
a report years ago saying that they had analyzed

00:05:56.830 --> 00:06:03.050
so-called threats of terrorists and found
that the measures put in place after 9/11,

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:09.450
which really cut back on a lot of our liberties
and certain constitutional rights that we

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:15.630
take for granted, they found no evidence that
it made us safer in any instance.

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:23.020
And I think the problem is most people don't
know that it's not publicized because in addition

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:27.880
to bolstering the claim that it does make
us safer, what it ultimately does is hand

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:33.330
over information that gives more control to
those in charge, and that means less autonomy

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:36.919
in our personal lives and more control by
others.

00:06:36.919 --> 00:06:42.939
Speaker 1: Now, if it let's talk about I have
nothing to hide, maybe maybe a little bit.

00:06:42.939 --> 00:06:45.880
A few years ago, I mean, that seemed pretty
reasonable to me.

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:52.909
You know, my my feeling was, yeah, I get that
there may be people who know more about me

00:06:52.909 --> 00:06:59.039
than I would willingly give up, but I don't
have any particular reason to worry about

00:06:59.039 --> 00:07:01.410
this because I don't have anything to hide.

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:05.310
And then I read about over criminalization
about how sometimes you may not think you

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:09.920
have anything to hide, but you may inadvertently
have done something or interacted with someone

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:14.530
who does maybe have something to hide and
then all of the other pernicious ways in which

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:15.830
this can be used.

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:20.410
What's the best sort of argument against that
idea of I really don't care, I don't have

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:21.419
anything to hide?

00:07:21.419 --> 00:07:26.490
Speaker 2: I think a fundamental argument
against that is that people need to realize

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:30.639
the importance that privacy has played in
this society.

00:07:30.639 --> 00:07:38.840
For example, during the AIDS epidemic, a lot
of health care providers hotlines realize

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:46.300
that in order to help quell the spread of
HIV, they would need to offer anonymous reporting

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:49.240
or anonymous testing.

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:51.110
And that's true in so many other segments.

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:58.660
So, for example, a woman who has been the
victim of spousal abuse has to know if she's

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:03.290
going to come forward to seek help, that she
can do so anonymously or privately.

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:08.139
And there are different levels of how you
shield one's identity, but that pertains to

00:08:08.139 --> 00:08:15.110
whistleblowing in government to encourage
people to come forward when they see malfeasance

00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:18.130
in the institutions in which we put our trust.

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:24.020
So I think you have to start at privacy plays
an essential part in society, and then you

00:08:24.020 --> 00:08:29.919
have to realize that if you're willing to
hand over your entire life to the government,

00:08:29.919 --> 00:08:32.810
we do so because we trust the government.

00:08:32.810 --> 00:08:36.169
And I think that's a wonderful premise on
which to base a democracy.

00:08:36.169 --> 00:08:42.810
But we have seen, in fact, that we cannot
always trust our leaders, that self-interest

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:50.380
or other factors may lead them to abuse our
information or very often just due to a casual

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:51.480
mistake.

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:54.500
My name may be confused with a similar name.

00:08:54.500 --> 00:09:02.389
I may be mixed up with someone who did commit
a crime, for example, but because of the databases

00:09:02.389 --> 00:09:08.910
that all of our information is stored in contain
so many errors, often just sloppy data entries,

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:14.310
that there have been so many cases of people
caught up in a crime that had nothing to do

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:17.600
with their lives and they were falsely prosecuted.

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:22.760
It also shows that if the government doesn't
like what you say, if you're a dissident or

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:28.360
just an outspoken individual who criticizes
a corporate policy, for example, or government

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:34.740
policy, they can target you and find just
about any reason among the many antiquated

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:37.430
laws that are on our books to go after you.

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:42.630
Speaker 1: Yeah, that I just I'm almost done
reading this new book, The Perfect Police

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:47.529
State, about China and the persecution of
of the wigger population.

00:09:47.529 --> 00:09:50.149
Is there a spectrum in the sense of it?

00:09:50.149 --> 00:09:55.070
It seems in terms of surveillance, China is
very much on one end.

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:56.290
What's on the other end?

00:09:56.290 --> 00:09:58.980
And where on that spectrum is, is the United
States?

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:02.250
In your assessment,
Speaker 2: that's an excellent question.

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:09.860
We do look to China as an example of the extreme
and what we as a purported democracy would

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:14.839
never want to be, where, for example, I just
have to say they have a system that is being

00:10:14.839 --> 00:10:20.940
used in some parts of China where you're under
such constant surveillance that if you jaywalk,

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:25.510
for example, or if you don't pick up after
your dog points are deducted.

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:26.510
Yes.

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:28.810
And that can reflect the electricity.

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:33.600
You get access to social services on the other
extreme.

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:39.660
And that's a great question, because I can't
really think of a nation on so many of our

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:47.100
partners in the so-called five eyes, New Zealand,
Canada, the UK have sort of rings of surveillance

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:48.100
everywhere.

00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:52.720
It's become the norm and we're becoming conditioned
to just accept that.

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:58.600
But I would imagine there are probably countries
in Scandinavia or countries maybe in Latin

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:07.100
America, South America, where the technology
in some of those countries may not be as sophisticated

00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:10.400
as what we have here with our robust economy.

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:15.529
So I would imagine that in countries without
that kind of surveillance accessibility, you

00:11:15.529 --> 00:11:18.120
don't have the digital surveillance we have
here.

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:22.880
Speaker 1: And so where is the US on this
spectrum right now in your assessment?

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:29.510
Speaker 2: I think the U.S. is pretty high
up there with England, for example, as basically

00:11:29.510 --> 00:11:36.829
having the equivalent of these what we call
rings of steel, the circles of surveillance

00:11:36.829 --> 00:11:39.160
cameras, closed circuit TV.

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:45.589
But we have that built into our economy so
that as simple as getting a cup of coffee

00:11:45.589 --> 00:11:50.190
may have a loyalty card where they track the
numbers of coffees that you buy, but they

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:53.959
also track more your credit card number, which
is linked to other data.

00:11:53.959 --> 00:12:01.759
I think that the US is in a race to perfect
total information awareness, the collection

00:12:01.759 --> 00:12:07.519
and with the entry of 5G, where more things
will be even connected and when they're Internet

00:12:07.519 --> 00:12:12.810
connected, that provides near perfect access
to our data.

00:12:12.810 --> 00:12:15.959
Speaker 1: I wonder, let me see how I can
kind of frame this up.

00:12:15.959 --> 00:12:21.639
I wonder if there's a degree of inevitability
to this because of the direction, not from

00:12:21.639 --> 00:12:26.000
a law enforcement standpoint, but from a sociocultural
technology standpoint.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:29.600
If there is a degree of inevitability to some
of this and I'll give you like one example

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:36.140
and then we can talk about it, the entire
literature now around longevity and living

00:12:36.140 --> 00:12:40.709
longer, healthier lives and nanomedicine and
customized.

00:12:40.709 --> 00:12:48.420
And it's mostly all premised on having much
more data, not only about you as an individual,

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:55.880
but data about how you compare to others and
what sort of diet or supplements or treatments

00:12:55.880 --> 00:12:57.190
or whatever the case may be.

00:12:57.190 --> 00:12:59.560
Good for you specifically.

00:12:59.560 --> 00:13:05.940
There's really no way it seems to do this
stuff without many more parties having access

00:13:05.940 --> 00:13:09.709
not only to our individual data, but to everybody
in some sense.

00:13:09.709 --> 00:13:13.980
And for the most part, I mean, in some kind
of like general sense, a lot of that stuff

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:18.920
sounds kind of interesting, like it would
be great to know what exact diet or what exact

00:13:18.920 --> 00:13:20.360
preventive treatment.

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:25.830
So is there some inevitability to this that
is going to be hard to restrict at all?

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:29.720
Speaker 2: I think that's absolutely right.

00:13:29.720 --> 00:13:36.720
First we have convenience, then we have the
kind of addictive quality that these devices

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:42.010
have and they're created, in effect, just
like video games to be addictive.

00:13:42.010 --> 00:13:48.370
Yeah, but there are, as with all technology,
so many positive benefits, as you mentioned.

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:55.920
And I think the trick is harnessing those,
but also having an educated public that is

00:13:55.920 --> 00:14:02.780
able to realize when they should have the
option of saying, I don't wish to share that.

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:07.769
And I think the problem that we're having
and why I wrote this book sort of is a primer

00:14:07.769 --> 00:14:14.100
so people can get a sense of the different
areas that were impacted by is we need to

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:18.720
first our legislators have to be more educated
about technology.

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:22.750
They're woefully behind the advancements.

00:14:22.750 --> 00:14:27.880
And that's a problem because they can't craft
laws that keep pace with new technologies

00:14:27.880 --> 00:14:31.260
that are emerging daily and new applications.

00:14:31.260 --> 00:14:38.170
So I think if we have a program where we educate
kids the first time they learn to type on

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:45.269
a keyboard what the perils are and what the
benefits are and really develop a healthy

00:14:45.269 --> 00:14:53.260
course in school that parents also participate
in in digital hygiene, we would not, for example,

00:14:53.260 --> 00:14:58.150
let a stranger walk into our bedroom every
night and use our toothbrush or just make

00:14:58.150 --> 00:14:59.440
themselves at.

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:03.829
Downstairs and that's in effect what we're
doing and what young kids are doing without

00:15:03.829 --> 00:15:04.829
that education.

00:15:04.829 --> 00:15:11.940
So I think you have to educate the public
and our leaders to the degree that they are

00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:18.480
able to say we need to build in privacy, for
example, into our devices or the software

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:24.940
has to be better tested before it's put on
the marketplace to ensure that adequate privacy

00:15:24.940 --> 00:15:27.470
protections are built into their infrastructure.

00:15:27.470 --> 00:15:33.509
Speaker 1: Last thing I want to touch on with
you, if someone right now is makes it a priority

00:15:33.509 --> 00:15:38.389
to prevent themselves from being tracked,
watched, monitored, surveilled, et cetera,

00:15:38.389 --> 00:15:43.970
you Google this and you see things like turn
off microphone access for your Facebook app

00:15:43.970 --> 00:15:44.970
on your phone.

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:45.970
Right.

00:15:45.970 --> 00:15:47.430
Very, very narrow and specific thing.

00:15:47.430 --> 00:15:52.950
All the way to don't use banks or credit cards,
which for the average participant in the modern

00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:56.170
economy is not at all sort of practical.

00:15:56.170 --> 00:16:00.949
To what degree is it possible to to really
not be tracked right now, regardless of how

00:16:00.949 --> 00:16:03.589
aggressively you're willing to adopt these
things?

00:16:03.589 --> 00:16:07.889
Or to some degree, if you're sort of like
living on the grid?

00:16:07.889 --> 00:16:09.820
It's quite difficult at this point in time.

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:12.470
Speaker 2: It's super difficult.

00:16:12.470 --> 00:16:18.079
One would have to literally not use a credit
card, stay off online searches.

00:16:18.079 --> 00:16:19.079
Yeah.

00:16:19.079 --> 00:16:24.730
And and and I think what's daunting to most
people, and I get it, is that, you know, encrypting

00:16:24.730 --> 00:16:31.620
for those who are reporters or journalists
who have to protect confidential sources,

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:34.000
there are wonderful measures in place that
they can use.

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:37.560
It becomes more difficult for the average
citizen to use all of those.

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:42.519
But there are basic things like not using
the same password over changing it, using

00:16:42.519 --> 00:16:45.259
a sentence instead of your pets name.

00:16:45.259 --> 00:16:49.190
There are basic things that aren't that hard,
enabling two factor authentication.

00:16:49.190 --> 00:16:55.240
For example, when you log into accounts, keep
down the number of apps that you have.

00:16:55.240 --> 00:17:00.430
They're really portals unless they come directly
from the manufacturer.

00:17:00.430 --> 00:17:04.360
Don't download download things from online.

00:17:04.360 --> 00:17:08.809
There are measures you can take to reduce
the amount of surveillance.

00:17:08.809 --> 00:17:13.390
I think it's unrealistic to say you can stop
it entirely unless you go off the grid.

00:17:13.390 --> 00:17:14.740
Speaker 1: Yeah, yeah.

00:17:14.740 --> 00:17:19.319
The book is I Have Nothing to Hide and 20
other myths about surveillance and privacy.

00:17:19.319 --> 00:17:24.350
We've been speaking with the book's author,
New York City based attorney Heidi Bogosian.

00:17:24.350 --> 00:17:25.490
Such a pleasure having you on.

00:17:25.490 --> 00:17:27.039
I really appreciate your time today.

00:17:27.039 --> 00:17:28.260
Speaker 2: Thank you, David.

